First, a quick summary of what’s happening:

1. In `.properties` files you can continue a value onto the next line by ending the line with a single backslash (`\`).  
2. Our current highlighter looks for **any** backslash + newline (via the regex `/\\\n/`) and treats that as “line-continuation,” so when you have two backslashes at the end of a line (i.e. a literal `\\` path ending in `\`), it still matches the _second_ backslash + newline and thinks it’s a continuation.  
3. We need to only match a backslash + newline when that backslash is **not** itself escaped—i.e. it must be a _single_ trailing backslash.  

The fix is to change the “escaped newline” pattern from a simple `/\\\n/` to one that uses a negative lookbehind, `/ (?<!\\)\\\n/`, so it only matches backslash + newline when that backslash is _not_ immediately preceded by another backslash.

Below is the one patch needed in `src/languages/properties.js`.

